Throttling vs Debouncing

Both are about controlling how often a function runs, especially when events happen too frequently (like typing, scrolling, window resize).

🔹 Debouncing

👉 Wait until user stops before running the function.

Example: Search box.

If the user types h, he, hel, hell, hello → API should run only once, after they stop typing (say 500ms).

Execution: Timer resets on every action. Runs only once after the last action.

📌 Code:

const debouncedSearch = debounce(() => console.log("API call"), 500);

input.addEventListener("keyup", debouncedSearch);

🔹 Throttling

👉 Run at most once in a fixed time, even if user keeps doing the action.

Example: Scroll event.

If user scrolls like crazy → function runs once per second, not on every pixel scroll.

Execution: Fires immediately, then waits X ms before running again. Stops when user stops.

📌 Code:

const throttledScroll = throttle(() => console.log("Scroll event"), 1000);

window.addEventListener("scroll", throttledScroll);


✅ Super Short Version (for interviews):

Debounce → Runs after user stops (good for search boxes).

Throttle → Runs at regular intervals while user continues (good for scroll/resize).

