an interviewer ask me that let suppose, you have 3 api request calling in react,one take 5 sec,one take 10 sec,one take 30 sec,how can i optimize the speed and his behaviour


✅ Ideal Answer to the Interviewer

If I have 3 API calls:

API A → 5 sec

API B → 10 sec

API C → 30 sec

1️⃣ Make All API Requests in Parallel (Not Sequential)

Many beginners mistakenly do:

await apiA();
await apiB();
await apiC();


This takes 45 seconds total.

To optimize, run them in parallel:

Promise.all([apiA(), apiB(), apiC()]);


Now total time is just 30 seconds (the longest one).

2️⃣ Show Partial UI Rendering (Don’t Wait for All)

Instead of blocking UI for 30 seconds, show data as soon as each API resolves.

You can use Promise.allSettled:

Promise.allSettled([apiA(), apiB(), apiC()]).then(results => {
  // Render data as it arrives
});


Or load them individually:

useEffect(() => {
  apiA().then(setAData);
  apiB().then(setBData);
  apiC().then(setCData);
}, []);


This improves perceived speed.

3️⃣ Add Caching (Avoid 30s wait on revisits)

Use caching like:

React Query / TanStack Query

SWR

LocalStorage caching

Service Worker

Example (React Query):

useQuery('bigData', apiC, {
  staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 min cache
});


Next visit → instant load.

4️⃣ Prioritize Requests (Like React Concurrency)

Not all data is equally important.

Load critical API (5s) first
Load non-critical API (30s) in background

apiA(); // critical
startTransition(() => apiC()); // less important

5️⃣ Abort Long Requests if User Leaves

Use AbortController to cancel the 30s request if component unmounts:

const controller = new AbortController();
fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });

return () => controller.abort();

6️⃣ Use Lazy Loading for the 30 sec API

If the 30s API is for some low-priority data (like reports, analytics):

Load only when user scrolls

Load after UI is interactive

Load on hover or click

🏆 Combined Final Strategy to Say in Interview

“I will run all API requests in parallel using Promise.all to reduce total time. I will not block UI — instead I will render data as soon as each API completes. I will prioritize critical APIs first, and load the long 30-second API in background or lazily. To improve performance on revisits, I will use caching (e.g., React Query). Also, I will abort the long request if the user navigates away to avoid wasted network.”

This shows:

✅ Technical knowledge
✅ UX thinking
✅ Best practices (React Query, concurrency, caching)


